WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.897
Take a look at this floor plan. This is a common situation in that you need to have multiple physical areas be on the same network.

00:09.897 --> 00:16.955
Now in this particular situation, you may think you need several levels of switches, such as two on each floor for these two networks,

00:16.955 --> 00:24.306
and then maybe another switch somewhere to where you can connect all of the operations people to each other and all of the project management people to each other.

00:24.306 --> 00:37.085
But, you don't. You could, if there aren't that many total users here, do all of this on one switch. You can create logical networks, VLANs, virtual LANs to accomplish this.

00:37.085 --> 00:46.623
You can do this through allocating some of the switch ports to operations and then the rest to project managers. This, in effect, creates two logical networks in one physical area.

00:46.623 --> 00:50.628
And if you do have too many users for one physical switch, no problem.

00:50.628 --> 00:59.528
You can add more physical switches as needed and still have the switches use VLANs to logically segment the network, through setting up trunk ports in between switches.

00:59.528 --> 01:05.898
In order for these two VLANs to talk to each other, though, you will need a router or a switch with routing capabilities,

01:05.898 --> 01:12.408
also known as a multilayer switch, and we will talk more about these devices a little later.

